{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09009","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09009","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09009","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marianna","familyName":"Nezhurina","name":"Nezhurina, Marianna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harsh","familyName":"Raj","name":"Raj, Harsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"May","name":"May, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jörg","familyName":"Franke","name":"Franke, Jörg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taishi","familyName":"Nakamura","name":"Nakamura, Taishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timur","familyName":"Carstensen","name":"Carstensen, Timur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niccolò","familyName":"Ajroldi","name":"Ajroldi, Niccolò","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ville","familyName":"Komulainen","name":"Komulainen, Ville","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Salinas","name":"Salinas, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jenia","familyName":"Jitsev","name":"Jitsev, Jenia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Open-sci-ref-0.02: open reference baselines for reproducible language model and dataset comparison"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T21:13:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:06:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T05:22:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:16:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-30T21:54:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-01T01:45:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-19T11:25:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-20T02:13:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reliable progress in language modeling depends on knowing whether a new dataset, architecture, or training recipe is genuinely better than existing alternatives. To foster reproducible learning procedure comparison, we present open-sci-ref, a family of dense transformers trained as open reference baselines at four scales (0.13B-1.7B parameters) on nine main reference datasets, with token budgets up to 1T, using a single tuned and fully documented training procedure. To facilitate a shared experimentation and measurement infrastructure, we release model weights, intermediate checkpoints, training logs, code, and downstream evaluations. When comparing training procedures, we take into account total compute spent. Placing training procedures on a common compute axis helps identify reference points that are weak or strong for the compute they use, without requiring a full scaling law study for every comparison. We identify Nemotron-CC-HQ as the strongest reference dataset on our evaluation suite: our 1.7B model trained on 1T of its tokens matches SmolLM2-1.7B with roughly 11$\\times$ less compute. Shared baselines expose pitfalls of scaling trend comparisons: when a small-scale point is unusually weak, the resulting slope can overstate the scalability of a training procedure. We illustrate this diagnostically with EuroLLM-1.7B, which underperforms 300B token FineWeb-Edu baseline despite 13$\\times$ more training compute. As an additional case study, the suite supports controlled comparison between permissively licensed and mixed-license datasets: the permissive-first MixtureVitae dataset changes rank substantially with scale. This also illustrates why data ranking should be evaluated through multi-scale reference trajectories rather than single proxy runs. All artifacts are publicly released."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v.1.2. Update on AAAI Workshop on Reproducible Artificial Intelligence (RAI, https://reproducibleai.github.io) 2026. Code for reproducing training, evaluation and raw experiments data is available at https://github.com/LAION-AI/open-sci-ref-0.02. Model weights and intermediate checkpoints are available at https://huggingface.co/collections/open-sci/open-sci-ref-002"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09009","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:23:46Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:23:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:15:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09121","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09121","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09121","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sophia","familyName":"Maria","name":"Maria, Sophia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compass-v3: Scaling Domain-Specific LLMs for Multilingual E-Commerce in Southeast Asia"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T03:23:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:13:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-16T03:29:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-18T00:01:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) excel in general-domain applications, yet their performance often degrades in specialized tasks requiring domain-specific knowledge. E-commerce is particularly challenging, as its data are noisy, heterogeneous, multilingual, and highly dynamic. We present Compass-v3, a vertical-domain Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) model with 245B total parameters and 71B active per token, designed for Southeast Asian e-commerce. Compass-v3 adopts fewer but larger experts, combined with hardware-efficient optimizations-such as intra-node expert parallelism and a customized memcpy operator-to maximize GPU utilization. The model is trained on 12T tokens of curated multilingual corpora and large-scale synthetic e-commerce instructions using a mixed-training strategy. To enhance alignment, we propose Optimal-Transport Direct Preference Optimization (OTPO), which captures token-level distinctions and improves instruction adherence in commerce-specific scenarios. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that Compass-v3 delivers state-of-the-art e-commerce performance, surpassing DeepSeek-V3.1, GPT-4 series, and Qwen3-235B. Moreover, Compass-v3 demonstrates strong multilingual capability across low-resource Southeast Asian languages (Indonesian, Thai, Filipino, Vietnamese, Malay, Taglog) and Portuguese while sustaining competitive performance on general benchmarks. It has already been widely applied in Shopee's industrial-scale e-commerce platform and is gradually replacing OpenAI's traffic, now accounting for over 70\\% of total LLM usage, highlighting its dual strengths in specialized commerce expertise and broad linguistic competence."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09121","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:26:25Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:26:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-18T02:44:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09505","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09505","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09505","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binglei","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Binglei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey T. H.","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Jeffrey T. H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwen","familyName":"Mo","name":"Mo, Zhiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Przemyslaw","familyName":"Forys","name":"Forys, Przemyslaw","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengyang","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Chengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timi","familyName":"Adeniran","name":"Adeniran, Timi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wayne","familyName":"Luk","name":"Luk, Wayne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongxiang","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Hongxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianyi","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy M.","familyName":"Jones","name":"Jones, Timothy M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rika","familyName":"Antonova","name":"Antonova, Rika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Mullins","name":"Mullins, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Combating the Memory Walls: Optimization Pathways for Long-Context Agentic LLM Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T14:49:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:40:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T11:31:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:43:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-12T10:29:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-14T01:00:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLMs now form the backbone of AI agents across a diverse range of applications, including tool use, command-line interfaces, and web or computer interaction. These agentic LLM inference tasks are fundamentally different from chatbot-focused inference. They often involve much longer context lengths to capture complex and prolonged inputs, such as an entire webpage DOM or complicated tool-call trajectories. This, in turn, generates significant off-chip memory traffic during inference and causes workloads to be constrained by two memory walls, namely the bandwidth wall and the capacity wall, preventing compute units from achieving high utilization.\n In this paper, we introduce PLENA, a hardware-software co-designed system built around three core optimization pathways. PLENA features a novel flattened systolic-array architecture (Pathway 1) and efficient compute and memory units that support an asymmetric quantization scheme (Pathway 2). It also provides native support for FlashAttention (Pathway 3). In addition, PLENA includes a complete software-hardware stack, consisting of a custom ISA, a compiler, a transaction-level simulator, and an automated design-space exploration flow. Experimental results show that PLENA delivers up to 2.23x and 4.70x higher throughput than the A100 GPU and TPU v6e, respectively, under identical multiplier counts and memory configurations during LLaMA agentic inference. PLENA also achieves up to 4.04x higher energy efficiency than the A100 GPU. The full PLENA system, including its simulator, compiler, ISA, and RTL implementation, will be open-sourced to the research community."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09505","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:35:33Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:35:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:17:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09116","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09116","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09116","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhao","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Junhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryohei","familyName":"Miyakawa","name":"Miyakawa, Ryohei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinpeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xinpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Risa","familyName":"Shinoda","name":"Shinoda, Risa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroaki","familyName":"Santo","name":"Santo, Hiroaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yosuke","familyName":"Toda","name":"Toda, Yosuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fumio","familyName":"Okura","name":"Okura, Fumio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Zero-shot Hierarchical Plant Segmentation via Foundation Segmentation Models and Text-to-image Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T02:53:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:12:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T10:47:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:39:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-19T05:20:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-22T01:18:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation segmentation models achieve reasonable leaf instance extraction from top-view crop images without training (i.e., zero-shot). However, segmenting entire plant individuals with each consisting of multiple overlapping leaves remains challenging. This problem is referred to as a hierarchical segmentation task, typically requiring annotated training datasets, which are often species-specific and require notable human labor. To address this, we introduce ZeroPlantSeg, a zero-shot segmentation for rosette-shaped plant individuals from top-view images. We integrate a foundation segmentation model, extracting leaf instances, and a vision-language model, reasoning about plants' structures to extract plant individuals without additional training. Evaluations on datasets with multiple plant species, growth stages, and shooting environments demonstrate that our method surpasses existing zero-shot methods and achieves better cross-domain performance than supervised methods. Implementations are available at https://github.com/JunhaoXing/ZeroPlantSeg."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"WACV 2026 Accepted"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09116","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:26:18Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:26:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-22T03:03:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09245","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09245","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09245","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuocheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shuocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silin","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Silin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Mengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeye","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yeye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shi","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Shi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongmei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dongmei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Jupiter: Enhancing LLM Data Analysis Capabilities via Notebook and Inference-Time Value-Guided Search"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T08:27:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:22:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-03T09:56:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-04T01:36:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have shown great promise in automating data science workflows, but existing models still struggle with multi-step reasoning and tool use, which limits their effectiveness on complex data analysis tasks. To address this, we propose a scalable pipeline that extracts high-quality, tool-based data analysis tasks and their executable multi-step solutions from real-world Jupyter notebooks and associated data files. Using this pipeline, we introduce NbQA, a large-scale dataset of standardized task-solution pairs that reflect authentic tool-use patterns in practical data science scenarios. To further enhance multi-step reasoning, we present Jupiter, a framework that formulates data analysis as a search problem and applies Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) to generate diverse solution trajectories for value model learning. During inference, Jupiter combines the value model and node visit counts to efficiently collect executable multi-step plans with minimal search steps. Experimental results show that Qwen2.5-7B and 14B-Instruct models on NbQA solve 77.82% and 86.38% of tasks on InfiAgent-DABench, respectively-matching or surpassing GPT-4o and advanced agent frameworks. Further evaluations demonstrate improved generalization and stronger tool-use reasoning across diverse multi-step reasoning tasks. Code and data are available at https://github.com/microsoft/Jupiter."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to AAAI 2026 (Main Technical Track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09245","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:29:23Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:29:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-04T18:40:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09387","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09387","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09387","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Bal-Ghaoui","name":"Bal-Ghaoui, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed","familyName":"Tiouti","name":"Tiouti, Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MetaLLMix : An XAI Aided LLM-Meta-learning Based Approach for Hyper-parameters Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T12:06:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:32:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T18:23:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:03:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T13:08:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:47:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective model and hyperparameter selection remains a major challenge in deep learning, often requiring extensive expertise and computation. While AutoML and large language models (LLMs) promise automation, current LLM-based approaches rely on trial and error and expensive APIs, which provide limited interpretability and generalizability. We propose MetaLLMiX, a zero-shot hyperparameter optimization framework combining meta-learning, explainable AI, and efficient LLM reasoning. By leveraging historical experiment outcomes with SHAP explanations, MetaLLMiX recommends optimal hyperparameters and pretrained models without additional trials. We further employ an LLM-as-judge evaluation to control output format, accuracy, and completeness. Experiments on eight medical imaging datasets using nine open-source lightweight LLMs show that MetaLLMiX achieves competitive or superior performance to traditional HPO methods while drastically reducing computational cost. Our local deployment outperforms prior API-based approaches, achieving optimal results on 5 of 8 tasks, response time reductions of 99.6-99.9%, and the fastest training times on 6 datasets (2.4-15.7x faster), maintaining accuracy within 1-5% of best-performing baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09387","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:32:46Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:32:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:25:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09372","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09372","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09372","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengxiang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Pengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingxiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lingxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyang","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Xinyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengxu","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Pengxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siteng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Siteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ru","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donglin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Donglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VLA-Adapter: An Effective Paradigm for Tiny-Scale Vision-Language-Action Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T11:42:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:31:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-22T17:22:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:49:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models typically bridge the gap between perceptual and action spaces by pre-training a large-scale Vision-Language Model (VLM) on robotic data. While this approach greatly enhances performance, it also incurs significant training costs. In this paper, we investigate how to effectively bridge vision-language (VL) representations to action (A). We introduce VLA-Adapter, a novel paradigm designed to reduce the reliance of VLA models on large-scale VLMs and extensive pre-training. To this end, we first systematically analyze the effectiveness of various VL conditions and present key findings on which conditions are essential for bridging perception and action spaces. Based on these insights, we propose a lightweight Policy module with Bridge Attention, which autonomously injects the optimal condition into the action space. In this way, our method achieves high performance using only a 0.5B-parameter backbone, without any robotic data pre-training. Extensive experiments on both simulated and real-world robotic benchmarks demonstrate that VLA-Adapter not only achieves state-of-the-art level performance, but also offers the fast inference speed reported to date. Furthermore, thanks to the proposed advanced bridging paradigm, VLA-Adapter enables the training of a powerful VLA model in just 8 hours on a single consumer-grade GPU, greatly lowering the barrier to deploying the VLA model. Project page: https://vla-adapter.github.io/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages; Project page: https://vla-adapter.github.io/; Github: https://github.com/OpenHelix-Team/VLA-Adapter; HuggingFace: https://huggingface.co/VLA-Adapter"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09372","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:32:25Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:32:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:55:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09595","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09595","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09595","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yikang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiwen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wenyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zekun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zekun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Wentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyuan","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Liyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingming","familyName":"Lao","name":"Lao, Mingming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingchao","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Yingchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaoqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Shen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu-Shen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Kling-Avatar: Grounding Multimodal Instructions for Cascaded Long-Duration Avatar Animation Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T16:34:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:46:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T04:08:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:20:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in audio-driven avatar video generation have significantly enhanced audio-visual realism. However, existing methods treat instruction conditioning merely as low-level tracking driven by acoustic or visual cues, without modeling the communicative purpose conveyed by the instructions. This limitation compromises their narrative coherence and character expressiveness. To bridge this gap, we introduce Kling-Avatar, a novel cascaded framework that unifies multimodal instruction understanding with photorealistic portrait generation. Our approach adopts a two-stage pipeline. In the first stage, we design a multimodal large language model (MLLM) director that produces a blueprint video conditioned on diverse instruction signals, thereby governing high-level semantics such as character motion and emotions. In the second stage, guided by blueprint keyframes, we generate multiple sub-clips in parallel using a first-last frame strategy. This global-to-local framework preserves fine-grained details while faithfully encoding the high-level intent behind multimodal instructions. Our parallel architecture also enables fast and stable generation of long-duration videos, making it suitable for real-world applications such as digital human livestreaming and vlogging. To comprehensively evaluate our method, we construct a benchmark of 375 curated samples covering diverse instructions and challenging scenarios. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Kling-Avatar is capable of generating vivid, fluent, long-duration videos at up to 1080p and 48 fps, achieving superior performance in lip synchronization accuracy, emotion and dynamic expressiveness, instruction controllability, identity preservation, and cross-domain generalization. These results establish Kling-Avatar as a new benchmark for semantically grounded, high-fidelity audio-driven avatar synthesis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Technical Report. Project Page: https://klingavatar.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09595","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:37:39Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:37:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:44:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09198","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09198","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09198","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Talia","familyName":"Sternberg","name":"Sternberg, Talia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"London","name":"London, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Omer","name":"Omer, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yossi","familyName":"Adi","name":"Adi, Yossi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GmSLM : Generative Marmoset Spoken Language Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:13:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:19:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T14:23:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:27:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Marmoset monkeys exhibit complex vocal communication, challenging the view that nonhuman primates vocal communication is entirely innate, and show similar features of human speech, such as vocal labeling of others and turn-taking. Studying their vocal communication offers a unique opportunity to link it with brain activity-especially given the difficulty of accessing the human brain in speech and language research. Since Marmosets communicate primarily through vocalizations, applying standard LLM approaches is not straightforward. We introduce Generative Marmoset Spoken Language Modeling (GmSLM), an optimized spoken language model pipeline for Marmoset vocal communication. We designed a novel zero-shot evaluation metrics using unsupervised in-the-wild data, alongside weakly labeled conversational data, to assess GmSLM and demonstrate its advantage over a basic human-speech-based baseline. GmSLM generated vocalizations closely matched real resynthesized samples acoustically and performed well on downstream tasks. Despite being fully unsupervised, GmSLM effectively distinguish real from artificial conversations and may support further investigations of the neural basis of vocal communication and provides a practical framework linking vocalization and brain activity. We believe GmSLM stands to benefit future work in neuroscience, bioacoustics, and evolutionary biology. Samples are provided under: pages.cs.huji.ac.il/adiyoss-lab/GmSLM."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09198","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:15Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:41:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09629","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09629","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09629","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Minghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengliang","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zhengliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiguang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengjie","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Pengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaochun","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Zhaochun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhumin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhumin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging the Capability Gap: Joint Alignment Tuning for Harmonizing LLM-based Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T17:15:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:47:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The advancement of large language models (LLMs) has enabled the construction of multi-agent systems to solve complex tasks by dividing responsibilities among specialized agents, such as a planning agent for subgoal generation and a grounding agent for executing tool-use actions. Most existing methods typically fine-tune these agents independently, leading to capability gaps among them with poor coordination. To address this, we propose MOAT, a Multi-Agent Joint Alignment Tuning framework that improves agents collaboration through iterative alignment. MOAT alternates between two key stages: (1) Planning Agent Alignment, which optimizes the planning agent to generate subgoal sequences that better guide the grounding agent; and (2) Grounding Agent Improving, which fine-tunes the grounding agent using diverse subgoal-action pairs generated by the agent itself to enhance its generalization capablity. Theoretical analysis proves that MOAT ensures a non-decreasing and progressively convergent training process. Experiments across six benchmarks demonstrate that MOAT outperforms state-of-the-art baselines, achieving average improvements of 3.1% on held-in tasks and 4.4% on held-out tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09629","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:38:27Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:38:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:38:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09541","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09541","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09541","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hala","familyName":"Hawashin","name":"Hawashin, Hala","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mina","familyName":"Abbaszadeh","name":"Abbaszadeh, Mina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Joseph","name":"Joseph, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beth","familyName":"Pearson","name":"Pearson, Beth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martha","familyName":"Lewis","name":"Lewis, Martha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehrnoosh","familyName":"sadrzadeh","name":"sadrzadeh, Mehrnoosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Compositional Concept Generalization with Variational Quantum Circuits"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T15:34:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:42:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Compositional generalization is a key facet of human cognition, but lacking in current AI tools such as vision-language models. Previous work examined whether a compositional tensor-based sentence semantics can overcome the challenge, but led to negative results. We conjecture that the increased training efficiency of quantum models will improve performance in these tasks. We interpret the representations of compositional tensor-based models in Hilbert spaces and train Variational Quantum Circuits to learn these representations on an image captioning task requiring compositional generalization. We used two image encoding techniques: a multi-hot encoding (MHE) on binary image vectors and an angle/amplitude encoding on image vectors taken from the vision-language model CLIP. We achieve good proof-of-concept results using noisy MHE encodings. Performance on CLIP image vectors was more mixed, but still outperformed classical compositional models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to: 2025 IEEE International Conference on Quantum Artificial Intelligence (QAI), Naples, Italy, Nov 2-5, 2025. This is the authors' accepted manuscript (AAM). An IEEE copyright notice appears on page 1. The final published version will appear in IEEE Xplore; DOI to be added when available"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09541","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:36:24Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:36:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:36:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09424","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09424","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09424","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyu","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zhiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maojiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Maojiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinwen","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Xinwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchuan","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yuchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaojing","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Shaojing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ENSI: Efficient Non-Interactive Secure Inference for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T13:04:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:34:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Secure inference enables privacy-preserving machine learning by leveraging cryptographic protocols that support computations on sensitive user data without exposing it. However, integrating cryptographic protocols with large language models (LLMs) presents significant challenges, as the inherent complexity of these protocols, together with LLMs' massive parameter scale and sophisticated architectures, severely limits practical usability. In this work, we propose ENSI, a novel non-interactive secure inference framework for LLMs, based on the principle of co-designing the cryptographic protocols and LLM architecture. ENSI employs an optimized encoding strategy that seamlessly integrates CKKS scheme with a lightweight LLM variant, BitNet, significantly reducing the computational complexity of encrypted matrix multiplications. In response to the prohibitive computational demands of softmax under homomorphic encryption (HE), we pioneer the integration of the sigmoid attention mechanism with HE as a seamless, retraining-free alternative. Furthermore, by embedding the Bootstrapping operation within the RMSNorm process, we efficiently refresh ciphertexts while markedly decreasing the frequency of costly bootstrapping invocations. Experimental evaluations demonstrate that ENSI achieves approximately an 8x acceleration in matrix multiplications and a 2.6x speedup in softmax inference on CPU compared to state-of-the-art method, with the proportion of bootstrapping is reduced to just 1%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09424","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:33:39Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:33:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:33:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09420","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09420","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09420","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haochen","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuzhang","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Shuzhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuangchen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shuangchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimin","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Dimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongzhong","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Hongzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runsheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Runsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HD-MoE: Hybrid and Dynamic Parallelism for Mixture-of-Expert LLMs with 3D Near-Memory Processing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T13:01:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:34:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) with Mixture-of-Expert (MoE) architectures achieve superior model performance with reduced computation costs, but at the cost of high memory capacity and bandwidth requirements. Near-Memory Processing (NMP) accelerators that stack memory directly on the compute through hybrid bonding have demonstrated high bandwidth with high energy efficiency, becoming a promising architecture for MoE models. However, as NMP accelerators comprise distributed memory and computation, how to map the MoE computation directly determines the LLM inference efficiency. Existing parallel mapping strategies, including Tensor Parallelism (TP) and Expert Parallelism (EP), suffer from either high communication costs or unbalanced computation utilization, leading to inferior efficiency. The dynamic routing mechanism of MoE LLMs further aggravates the efficiency challenges. Therefore, in this paper, we propose HD-MoE to automatically optimize the MoE parallel computation across an NMP accelerator. HD-MoE features an offline automatic hybrid parallel mapping algorithm and an online dynamic scheduling strategy to reduce the communication costs while maximizing the computation utilization. With extensive experimental results, we demonstrate that HD-MoE achieves a speedup ranging from 1.1x to 1.8x over TP, 1.1x to 1.5x over EP, and 1.0x to 1.4x over the baseline Hybrid TP-EP with Compute-Balanced parallelism strategies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 15 figures, International Conference on Computer-Aided Design (ICCAD) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09420","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:33:33Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:33:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:33:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09321","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09321","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09321","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hangyi","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Hangyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxi","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Yuxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanwen","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Hanwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xinhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Adaptive ML Benchmarks: Web-Agent-Driven Construction, Domain Expansion, and Metric Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T10:10:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:27:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled the emergence of general-purpose agents for automating end-to-end machine learning (ML) workflows, including data analysis, feature engineering, model training, and competition solving. However, existing benchmarks remain limited in task coverage, domain diversity, difficulty modeling, and evaluation rigor, failing to capture the full capabilities of such agents in realistic settings. We present TAM Bench, a diverse, realistic, and structured benchmark for evaluating LLM-based agents on end-to-end ML tasks. TAM Bench features three key innovations: (1) A browser automation and LLM-based task acquisition system that automatically collects and structures ML challenges from platforms such as Kaggle, AIcrowd, and Biendata, spanning multiple task types and data modalities (e.g., tabular, text, image, graph, audio); (2) A leaderboard-driven difficulty modeling mechanism that estimates task complexity using participant counts and score dispersion, enabling scalable and objective task calibration; (3) A multi-dimensional evaluation framework incorporating performance, format compliance, constraint adherence, and task generalization. Based on 150 curated AutoML tasks, we construct three benchmark subsets of different sizes -- Lite, Medium, and Full -- designed for varying evaluation scenarios. The Lite version, with 18 tasks and balanced coverage across modalities and difficulty levels, serves as a practical testbed for daily benchmarking and comparative studies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09321","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:31:13Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:31:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:31:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09303","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09303","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09303","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grazia Sveva","familyName":"Ascione","name":"Ascione, Grazia Sveva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolò","familyName":"Tamagnone","name":"Tamagnone, Nicolò","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From scratch to silver: Creating trustworthy training data for patent-SDG classification using Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T09:44:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:26:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Classifying patents by their relevance to the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) is crucial for tracking how innovation addresses global challenges. However, the absence of a large, labeled dataset limits the use of supervised learning. Existing methods, such as keyword searches, transfer learning, and citation-based heuristics, lack scalability and generalizability. This paper frames patent-to-SDG classification as a weak supervision problem, using citations from patents to SDG-tagged scientific publications (NPL citations) as a noisy initial signal. To address its sparsity and noise, we develop a composite labeling function (LF) that uses large language models (LLMs) to extract structured concepts, namely functions, solutions, and applications, from patents and SDG papers based on a patent ontology. Cross-domain similarity scores are computed and combined using a rank-based retrieval approach. The LF is calibrated via a custom positive-only loss that aligns with known NPL-SDG links without penalizing discovery of new SDG associations. The result is a silver-standard, soft multi-label dataset mapping patents to SDGs, enabling the training of effective multi-label regression models. We validate our approach through two complementary strategies: (1) internal validation against held-out NPL-based labels, where our method outperforms several baselines including transformer-based models, and zero-shot LLM; and (2) external validation using network modularity in patent citation, co-inventor, and co-applicant graphs, where our labels reveal greater thematic, cognitive, and organizational coherence than traditional technological classifications. These results show that weak supervision and semantic alignment can enhance SDG classification at scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09303","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:30:49Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:30:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:30:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09267","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09267","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09267","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linhao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Linhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwen","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Yiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ziyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unified Start, Personalized End: Progressive Pruning for Efficient 3D Medical Image Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T08:53:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:24:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D medical image segmentation often faces heavy resource and time consumption, limiting its scalability and rapid deployment in clinical environments. Existing efficient segmentation models are typically static and manually designed prior to training, which restricts their adaptability across diverse tasks and makes it difficult to balance performance with resource efficiency. In this paper, we propose PSP-Seg, a progressive pruning framework that enables dynamic and efficient 3D segmentation. PSP-Seg begins with a redundant model and iteratively prunes redundant modules through a combination of block-wise pruning and a functional decoupling loss. We evaluate PSP-Seg on five public datasets, benchmarking it against seven state-of-the-art models and six efficient segmentation models. Results demonstrate that the lightweight variant, PSP-Seg-S, achieves performance on par with nnU-Net while reducing GPU memory usage by 42-45%, training time by 29-48%, and parameter number by 83-87% across all datasets. These findings underscore PSP-Seg's potential as a cost-effective yet high-performing alternative for widespread clinical application."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09267","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:29:56Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:29:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:29:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09226","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09226","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09226","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haipeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constructing a Question-Answering Simulator through the Distillation of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:59:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:21:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The question-answering (QA) simulator is a model that mimics real student learning behaviors and predicts their correctness of their responses to questions. QA simulators enable educational recommender systems (ERS) to collect large amounts of training data without interacting with real students, thereby preventing harmful recommendations made by an undertrained ERS from undermining actual student learning. Given the QA history, there are two categories of solutions to predict the correctness, conducting the simulation: (1) LLM-free methods, which apply a traditional sequential model to transfer the QA history into a vector representation first, and make predictions based on the representation; (2) LLM-based methods, which leverage the domain knowledge and reasoning capability of LLM to enhence the prediction. LLM-free methods offer fast inference but generally yield suboptimal performance. In contrast, most LLM-based methods achieve better results, but at the cost of slower inference speed and higher GPU memory consumption. In this paper, we propose a method named LLM Distillation based Simulator (LDSim), which distills domain knowledge and reasoning capability from an LLM to better assist prediction, thereby improving simulation performance. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our LDSim achieves strong results on both the simulation task and the knowledge tracing (KT) task. Our code is publicly available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/LDSim-05A9."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09226","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:56Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:28:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09193","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09193","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09193","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxiang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Haoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanhui","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Yanhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjiao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Wenjiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruichen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinqiu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yinqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dusit","familyName":"Niyato","name":"Niyato, Dusit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongfang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Hongfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abbas","familyName":"Jamalipour","name":"Jamalipour, Abbas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwen","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Shiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AI Reasoning for Wireless Communications and Networking: A Survey and Perspectives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T07:09:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:19:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Artificial Intelligence (AI) techniques play a pivotal role in optimizing wireless communication networks. However, traditional deep learning approaches often act as closed boxes, lacking the structured reasoning abilities needed to tackle complex, multi-step decision problems. This survey provides a comprehensive review and outlook of reasoning-enabled AI in wireless communication networks, with a focus on Large Language Models (LLMs) and other advanced reasoning paradigms. In particular, LLM-based agents can combine reasoning with long-term planning, memory, tool utilization, and autonomous cross-layer control to dynamically optimize network operations with minimal human intervention. We begin by outlining the evolution of intelligent wireless networking and the limitations of conventional AI methods. We then introduce emerging AI reasoning techniques. Furthermore, we establish a classification system applicable to wireless network tasks. We also present a layer-by-layer examination for AI reasoning, covering the physical, data link, network, transport, and application layers. For each part, we identify key challenges and illustrate how AI reasoning methods can improve AI-based wireless communication performance. Finally, we discuss key research directions for AI reasoning toward future wireless communication networks. By combining insights from both communications and AI, this survey aims to chart a path for integrating reasoning techniques into the next-generation wireless networks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09193","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:28:08Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:28:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:28:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09174","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09174","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09174","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanchen","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Zhanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangnan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xiangnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiqi","familyName":"Kou","name":"Kou, Kaiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benyou","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Benyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haizhou","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EchoX: Towards Mitigating Acoustic-Semantic Gap via Echo Training for Speech-to-Speech LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T06:17:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:18:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speech-to-speech large language models (SLLMs) are attracting increasing attention. Derived from text-based large language models (LLMs), SLLMs often exhibit degradation in knowledge and reasoning capabilities. We hypothesize that this limitation arises because current training paradigms for SLLMs fail to bridge the acoustic-semantic gap in the feature representation space. To address this issue, we propose EchoX, which leverages semantic representations and dynamically generates speech training targets. This approach integrates both acoustic and semantic learning, enabling EchoX to preserve strong reasoning abilities as a speech LLM. Experimental results demonstrate that EchoX, with about six thousand hours of training data, achieves advanced performance on multiple knowledge-based question-answering benchmarks. The project is available at https://github.com/FreedomIntelligence/EchoX."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09174","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:27:42Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:27:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:27:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09097","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09097","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09097","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honghui","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Honghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiva","familyName":"Shrestha","name":"Shrestha, Shiva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhipeng","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Zhipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DP-FedLoRA: Privacy-Enhanced Federated Fine-Tuning for On-Device Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T02:16:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:11:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As on-device large language model (LLM) systems become increasingly prevalent, federated fine-tuning enables advanced language understanding and generation directly on edge devices; however, it also involves processing sensitive, user-specific data, raising significant privacy concerns within the federated learning framework. To address these challenges, we propose DP-FedLoRA, a privacy-enhanced federated fine-tuning framework that integrates LoRA-based adaptation with differential privacy in a communication-efficient setting. Each client locally clips and perturbs its LoRA matrices using Gaussian noise to satisfy ($ε$, $δ$)-differential privacy. We further provide a theoretical analysis demonstrating the unbiased nature of the updates and deriving bounds on the variance introduced by noise, offering practical guidance for privacy-budget calibration. Experimental results across mainstream benchmarks show that DP-FedLoRA delivers competitive performance while offering strong privacy guarantees, paving the way for scalable and privacy-preserving LLM deployment in on-device environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09097","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:25:48Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:25:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:25:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09091","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09091","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09091","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honglan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Honglan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yibin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feifei","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Feifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haihui","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Haihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyan","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Xiaoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Confidential and Efficient LLM Inference with Dual Privacy Protection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T01:54:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:11:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"CPU-based trusted execution environments (TEEs) and differential privacy (DP) have gained wide applications for private inference. Due to high inference latency in TEEs, researchers use partition-based approaches that offload linear model components to GPUs. However, dense nonlinear layers of large language models (LLMs) result in significant communication overhead between TEEs and GPUs. DP-based approaches apply random noise to protect data privacy, but this compromises LLM performance and semantic understanding. To overcome the above drawbacks, this paper proposes CMIF, a Confidential and efficient Model Inference Framework. CMIF confidentially deploys the embedding layer in the client-side TEE and subsequent layers on GPU servers. Meanwhile, it optimizes the Report-Noisy-Max mechanism to protect sensitive inputs with a slight decrease in model performance. Extensive experiments on Llama-series models demonstrate that CMIF reduces additional inference overhead in TEEs while preserving user data privacy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by DASFAA2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09091","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:25:40Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:25:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:25:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09066","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09066","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09066","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haowei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Haowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yushang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sitao","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Sitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Instructional Prompt Optimization for Few-Shot LLM-Based Recommendations on Cold-Start Users"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T00:13:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:09:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The cold-start user issue further compromises the effectiveness of recommender systems in limiting access to the historical behavioral information. It is an effective pipeline to optimize instructional prompts on a few-shot large language model (LLM) used in recommender tasks. We introduce a context-conditioned prompt formulation method P(u,\\ Ds)\\ \\rightarrow\\ R\\widehat, where u is a cold-start user profile, Ds is a curated support set, and R\\widehat is the predicted ranked list of items. Based on systematic experimentation with transformer-based autoregressive LLMs (BioGPT, LLaMA-2, GPT-4), we provide empirical evidence that optimal exemplar injection and instruction structuring can significantly improve the precision@k and NDCG scores of such models in low-data settings. The pipeline uses token-level alignments and embedding space regularization with a greater semantic fidelity. Our findings not only show that timely composition is not merely syntactic but also functional as it is in direct control of attention scales and decoder conduct through inference. This paper shows that prompt-based adaptation may be considered one of the ways to address cold-start recommendation issues in LLM-based pipelines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09066","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:25:04Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:25:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:25:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09058","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09058","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09058","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ajay","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Ajay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Praveen","familyName":"Rao","name":"Rao, Praveen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Sanders","name":"Sanders, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimizing the Variant Calling Pipeline Execution on Human Genomes Using GPU-Enabled Machines"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T23:40:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:08:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Variant calling is the first step in analyzing a human genome and aims to detect variants in an individual's genome compared to a reference genome. Due to the computationally-intensive nature of variant calling, genomic data are increasingly processed in cloud environments as large amounts of compute and storage resources can be acquired with the pay-as-you-go pricing model. In this paper, we address the problem of efficiently executing a variant calling pipeline for a workload of human genomes on graphics processing unit (GPU)-enabled machines. We propose a novel machine learning (ML)-based approach for optimizing the workload execution to minimize the total execution time. Our approach encompasses two key techniques: The first technique employs ML to predict the execution times of different stages in a variant calling pipeline based on the characteristics of a genome sequence. Using the predicted times, the second technique generates optimal execution plans for the machines by drawing inspiration from the flexible job shop scheduling problem. The plans are executed via careful synchronization across different machines. We evaluated our approach on a workload of publicly available genome sequences using a testbed with different types of GPU hardware. We observed that our approach was effective in predicting the execution times of variant calling pipeline stages using ML on features such as sequence size, read quality, percentage of duplicate reads, and average read length. In addition, our approach achieved 2X speedup (on an average) over a greedy approach that also used ML for predicting the execution times on the tested workload of sequences. Finally, our approach achieved 1.6X speedup (on an average) over a dynamic approach that executed the workload based on availability of resources without using any ML-based time predictions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in 14th International Workshop on Parallel and AI-based Bioinformatics and Biomedicine (ParBio), Philadelphia, 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09058","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:24:53Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:24:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:24:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09055","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09055","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09055","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piyush","familyName":"Pant","name":"Pant, Piyush","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving LLM Safety and Helpfulness using SFT and DPO: A Study on OPT-350M"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T23:22:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:08:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This research investigates the effectiveness of alignment techniques, Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT), Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), and a combined SFT+DPO approach on improving the safety and helpfulness of the OPT-350M language model. Utilizing the Anthropic Helpful-Harmless RLHF dataset, we train and evaluate four models: the base OPT350M, an SFT model, a DPO model, and a model trained with both SFT and DPO. We introduce three key evaluation metrics: Harmlessness Rate (HmR), Helpfulness Rate (HpR), and a Combined Alignment Score (CAS), all derived from reward model outputs. The results show that while SFT outperforms DPO, The combined SFT+DPO model outperforms all others across all metrics, demonstrating the complementary nature of these techniques. Our findings also highlight challenges posed by noisy data, limited GPU resources, and training constraints. This study offers a comprehensive view of how fine-tuning strategies affect model alignment and provides a foundation for more robust alignment pipelines in future work."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 3 figures. Code and dataset available at https://github.com/PiyushWithPant/Improving-LLM-Safety-and-Helpfulness-using-SFT-and-DPO"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09055","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:24:49Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:24:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:24:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08973","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08973","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08973","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshit","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Harshit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ari","familyName":"Hietanen","name":"Hietanen, Ari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simo","familyName":"Särkkä","name":"Särkkä, Simo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ultrafast Deep Learning-Based Scatter Estimation in Cone-Beam Computed Tomography"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T20:07:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:05:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Purpose: Scatter artifacts drastically degrade the image quality of cone-beam computed tomography (CBCT) scans. Although deep learning-based methods show promise in estimating scatter from CBCT measurements, their deployment in mobile CBCT systems or edge devices is still limited due to the large memory footprint of the networks. This study addresses the issue by applying networks at varying resolutions and suggesting an optimal one, based on speed and accuracy.\n Methods: First, the reconstruction error in down-up sampling of CBCT scatter signal was examined at six resolutions by comparing four interpolation methods. Next, a recent state-of-the-art method was trained across five image resolutions and evaluated for the reductions in floating-point operations (FLOPs), inference times, and GPU memory requirements.\n Results: Reducing the input size and network parameters achieved a 78-fold reduction in FLOPs compared to the baseline method, while maintaining comarable performance in terms of mean-absolute-percentage-error (MAPE) and mean-square-error (MSE). Specifically, the MAPE decreased to 3.85% compared to 4.42%, and the MSE decreased to 1.34 \\times 10^{-2} compared to 2.01 \\times 10^{-2}. Inference time and GPU memory usage were reduced by factors of 16 and 12, respectively. Further experiments comparing scatter-corrected reconstructions on a large, simulated dataset and real CBCT scans from water and Sedentex CT phantoms clearly demonstrated the robustness of our method.\n Conclusion: This study highlights the underappreciated role of downsampling in deep learning-based scatter estimation. The substantial reduction in FLOPs and GPU memory requirements achieved by our method enables scatter correction in resource-constrained environments, such as mobile CBCT and edge devices."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08973","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:22:55Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:22:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:22:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08913","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08913","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08913","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sin-Yu","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Sin-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent W. S.","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Vincent W. S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalized User-Oriented Image Semantic Coding Empowered by Large Vision-Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T18:18:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:01:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Semantic communication has shown outstanding performance in preserving the overall source information in wireless transmission. For semantically rich content such as images, human users are often interested in specific regions depending on their intent. Moreover, recent semantic coding models are mostly trained on specific datasets. However, real-world applications may involve images out of the distribution of training dataset, which makes generalization a crucial but largely unexplored problem. To incorporate user's intent into semantic coding, in this paper, we propose a generalized user-oriented image semantic coding (UO-ISC) framework, where the user provides a text query indicating its intent. The transmitter extracts features from the source image which are relevant to the user's query. The receiver reconstructs an image based on those features. To enhance the generalization ability, we integrate contrastive language image pre-training (CLIP) model, which is a pretrained large vision-language model (VLM), into our proposed UO-ISC framework. To evaluate the relevance between the reconstructed image and the user's query, we introduce the user-intent relevance loss, which is computed by using a pretrained large VLM, large language-and-vision assistant (LLaVA) model. When performing zero-shot inference on unseen objects, simulation results show that the proposed UO-ISC framework outperforms the state-of-the-art query-aware image semantic coding in terms of the answer match rate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by IEEE Global Communications Conference (GLOBECOM), Taipei, Taiwan, Dec. 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08913","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:21:32Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:21:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:21:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08867","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.08867","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.08867","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K.","familyName":"Pronk","name":"Pronk, K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Q.","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmarking Energy Efficiency of Large Language Models Using vLLM"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T01","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T11:03:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:00:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The prevalence of Large Language Models (LLMs) is having an growing impact on the climate due to the substantial energy required for their deployment and use. To create awareness for developers who are implementing LLMs in their products, there is a strong need to collect more information about the energy efficiency of LLMs. While existing research has evaluated the energy efficiency of various models, these benchmarks often fall short of representing realistic production scenarios. In this paper, we introduce the LLM Efficiency Benchmark, designed to simulate real-world usage conditions. Our benchmark utilizes vLLM, a high-throughput, production-ready LLM serving backend that optimizes model performance and efficiency. We examine how factors such as model size, architecture, and concurrent request volume affect inference energy efficiency. Our findings demonstrate that it is possible to create energy efficiency benchmarks that better reflect practical deployment conditions, providing valuable insights for developers aiming to build more sustainable AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08867","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-12T01:20:27Z","registered":"2025-09-12T01:20:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:20:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":27,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+LLM+OR+GPU%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+LLM+OR+GPU%29%29+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28inference+OR+communication+OR+kernel+OR+serving+OR+parallel+OR+training%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28inference+OR+communication+OR+kernel+OR+serving+OR+parallel+OR+training%29%29+AND+registered%3A%5B2025-09-11T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-12T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026sort=registered"}}